<div class="section-label">Step 3: Know Your Legal Rights</div>
<h2>Corridor Preservation vs. Eminent Domain</h2>

  <p><strong>UDOT can acquire property through different processes — and your rights differ depending on which one applies:</strong> This page is not legal advice. If UDOT contacts you or your property appears in the Draft State Environmental Study right-of-way, review UDOT's acquisition guidance and consult a qualified real estate or eminent-domain attorney before signing documents.{% cite "udot-row-acquisition" %}</p>

<h3>Corridor Preservation (Current Status — Right Now)</h3>
<ul style="margin: 16px 0 20px 24px; line-height: 2.2;">
  <li><strong>Voluntary process only.</strong> UDOT cannot force acquisition on unfunded projects.</li>
  <li>If UDOT offers to purchase your property, <strong>you can decline</strong> or negotiate price.</li>
  <li>You receive fair market value based on certified appraisal.</li>
  <li>You can commission an independent appraisal at UDOT's expense and negotiate.</li>
  <li>Relocation assistance covers moving costs and temporary housing if applicable.</li>
  <li><strong>Key right:</strong> You control whether you sell. Say "no" and UDOT must stop pursuing you (unless project becomes funded).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Eminent Domain (Post-Funding — If/When Project is Funded)</h3>
<ul style="margin: 16px 0 20px 24px; line-height: 2.2;">
  <li><strong>Mandatory acquisition.</strong> Once project is funded and right-of-way is determined, UDOT can force acquisition.</li>
  <li>You still have rights:
    <ul style="margin: 10px 0 10px 24px;">
      <li>Receive UDOT appraisal (you can dispute it)</li>
      <li>Commission independent appraisal at UDOT's expense</li>
      <li>Negotiate the compensation</li>
      <li>Seek legal review or challenge compensation through the process available under Utah law</li>
      <li>Receive relocation assistance covering full moving costs</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
   <li><strong>Key right:</strong> You may not be able to stop acquisition after funding and final right-of-way approval, but you can seek independent advice and dispute compensation.</li>
</ul>

