<div class="section-label">Step 5: Take Action Now</div>
<h2>Checklist for Property Owners</h2>

<div style="margin: 20px 0;">
  <p><strong>Register for Updates (5 minutes):</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 10px 24px;">
    <li>☐ Visit <a href="https://udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension" rel="noopener noreferrer">udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension</a></li>
    <li>☐ Sign up for State Environmental Study email updates</li>
    <li>☐ Add to calendar: Fall 2026 for Draft State Environmental Study release</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Document Your Property (1-2 hours):</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 10px 24px;">
    <li>☐ Take photos and video of property (interior, exterior, condition)</li>
    <li>☐ Get property survey if you don't have one</li>
    <li>☐ Request title search from county assessor or attorney</li>
    <li>☐ Note any unique features (trees, structures, views) that add value</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Get Financial Information (2-3 hours):</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 10px 24px;">
    <li>☐ Get mortgage payoff amount from lender</li>
    <li>☐ Collect property tax records (last 5 years)</li>
    <li>☐ Note any recent improvements or repairs</li>
    <li>☐ Consider getting voluntary appraisal now (compare to UDOT appraisal later)</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Make a Decision (Ongoing):</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 10px 24px;">
    <li>☐ Review options (wait, sell now, or prepare to negotiate)</li>
    <li>☐ If UDOT approaches you, don't accept first offer — get independent appraisal</li>
    <li>☐ Consult an attorney if UDOT's appraisal or proposed terms seem unfair</li>
    <li>☐ Participate in public comment periods during State Environmental Study (Fall 2026)</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Contact Key People:</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 10px 24px;">
    <li>☐ <strong>UDOT State Environmental Study Project Team:</strong> <a href="https://udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension" rel="noopener noreferrer">udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension</a> (questions about alignment, right-of-way, property impacts)</li>
    <li>☐ <strong>West Haven City Council:</strong> <a href="https://www.westhavenut.gov/government/city_council/agendas_and_minutes.php" rel="noopener noreferrer">westhavenut.gov agendas and minutes</a> (local government liaison)</li>
    <li>☐ <strong>Weber County Planning:</strong> <a href="https://webercountyutah.gov/planning" rel="noopener noreferrer">webercountyutah.gov/planning</a> (corridor preservation coordination)</li>
    <li>☐ <strong>Your elected representative</strong> (state legislator, city council member) — they can influence funding prioritization</li>
    <li>☐ <strong>Real estate attorney</strong> (if property affected and you need legal advice)</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>Remember:</strong> The timeline for Phase 3 is long (2043–2050 official plan, possibly 2032–2036 if accelerated). You have time to gather information, prepare, and make informed decisions. The key is to stay informed and act before the project enters funded construction phase.</p>
</div>

